    "title": "Jared Kraham is re-elected City of Binghamton mayor for second consecutive term - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Riccardo Monico ,\n1 days ago Binghamton residents voted to re-elect Mayor Jared Kraham to a second term on Election Day, Nov. 4.\nKraham, a Republican, beat Democratic challenger Miles Burnett with nearly 54% of the vote. According to the Broome County Board of Elections, Kraham had 4,592 votes and Burnett had 3,896 with all precincts reporting.\nKraham has served as Binghamton's mayor for the past four years after serving as deputy mayor under Richard David.\nKraham listed public safety as a top priority for his campaign, stating he would continue to implement policies to reduce violent crime, increase police staffing and expand mental health services available at the dispatch level. Kraham also emphasized increasing housing stock throughout the city, as well as improving neighborhood quality of life through initiatives like zoning and blight removal.\nElection Day: Here's the latest 2025 news, results, live updates from Binghamton NY\nPrior to running for mayor, Burnett served as deputy chief of staff for New York State Senator Lea Webb, and has held leadership roles with nonprofits and community organizations.\nThroughout his campaign, Burnett said he would focus on providing quality affordable housing to Binghamton residents, including establishing a plan to construct 2,500 new units of affordable workforce and senior housing over the next decade. Burnett also said he would prioritize a holistic approach to public safety including the hiring of mental health experts, and expand youth services.\nThis article originally appeared on Binghamton Press & Sun-Bulletin: Jared Kraham is re-elected City of Binghamton mayor for second consecutive term\n14",
